1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:04,840
How can blood get past a narrowed heart
artery while the narrowing is still there?

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:06,740
Watch it take a new route.

3
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:12,740
This is coronary artery bypass grafting,
often called CABG or heart bypass

4
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:18,220
surgery. A surgeon uses a blood vessel
from elsewhere in the body to carry blood

5
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:22,280
to a coronary artery beyond a narrowed or
blocked section.

6
00:00:23,460 --> 00:00:27,040
The heart muscle downstream can receive
blood through that new route.

7
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:31,380
The coronary arteries bring oxygen-rich
blood to the heart muscle.

8
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:35,060
Over time, plaque can narrow their
openings.

9
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:39,860
The muscle may still receive enough blood
at rest, yet have too little when it is

10
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:44,760
working harder. This can cause angina,
which often feels like pressure or

11
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:46,120
discomfort in the chest.

12
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:51,980
The team reviews where the disease is, how
much heart muscle it affects and how well

13
00:00:52,020 --> 00:00:56,220
the heart pumps. An angiogram shows the
artery openings.

14
00:00:56,900 --> 00:01:01,260
An ultrasound of the heart can show how
well the main pumping chamber works.

15
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:07,080
When coronary disease is stable, medicines
and heart-healthy habits help manage

16
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:09,360
symptoms and reduce future risk.

17
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:14,460
If angina still limits daily life and
there is a significant narrowing that can

18
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:18,560
be treated, restoring blood flow can help
relieve symptoms.

19
00:01:19,740 --> 00:01:24,540
One option is PCI, often called
angioplasty and stenting.

20
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:28,060
It opens a selected section from inside
the artery.

21
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:33,080
Bypass surgery supplies blood around a
narrowing through a separate vessel.

22
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:36,060
Some people continue with medicines alone.

23
00:01:36,980 --> 00:01:41,360
Others have PCI or bypass surgery
alongside medical care.

24
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:45,700
The right choice depends on what the
treatment is expected to achieve for that

25
00:01:45,740 --> 00:01:46,180
person.

26
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:52,120
The left main coronary artery supplies two
major branches that feed a large part of

27
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:57,360
the heart. When the left main is
significantly narrowed, bypass can improve

28
00:01:57,440 --> 00:02:00,200
survival compared with medical treatment
alone.

29
00:02:01,220 --> 00:02:05,700
PCI can be a reasonable option for some
people whose arteries can be treated

30
00:02:05,860 --> 00:02:08,120
equally well with either procedure.

31
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:14,080
Another example is diabetes with disease
in several coronary arteries, including

32
00:02:14,140 --> 00:02:17,800
the left anterior descending artery, or
LAD.

33
00:02:18,720 --> 00:02:21,100
The LAD runs down the front of the heart.

34
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:25,940
Bypass is often favoured when those
patients are suitable for surgery.

35
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:31,040
In suitable patients with disease in
several arteries and a severely weakened

36
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:34,720
left ventricle, bypass can also improve
survival.

37
00:02:35,860 --> 00:02:39,940
The team looks at the location and
complexity of the disease as well as the

38
00:02:39,980 --> 00:02:41,120
number of narrowings.

39
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:45,400
A heart attack can suddenly cut blood flow
to heart muscle.

40
00:02:46,260 --> 00:02:51,620
In one type, called STEMI, the immediate
priority is to restore blood flow quickly.

41
00:02:52,620 --> 00:02:57,820
When it is available promptly and can be
done, emergency PCI is usually the

42
00:02:57,860 --> 00:03:02,760
first choice. Urgent bypass can help
selected people when PCI is

43
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:07,740
impossible or unsuccessful and a large
area of heart muscle is still at risk.

44
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:12,720
The team checks whether there are suitable
arteries to attach the grafts to and

45
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:14,760
whether surgery is likely to help.

46
00:03:15,580 --> 00:03:20,240
In some people whose emergency artery has
already been reopened, bypass can be

47
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:22,900
considered later for other complex
narrowings.

48
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:27,400
The timing depends on the findings and on
how stable the person is.

49
00:03:28,260 --> 00:03:31,800
When the best approach is unclear, the
Heart Team can bring together an

50
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:36,440
interventional cardiologist, a cardiac
surgeon and other clinicians involved in

51
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:41,480
the person’s care. They look at the
angiogram, how well the heart pumps,

52
00:03:41,820 --> 00:03:44,920
and health factors such as kidney disease
or frailty.

53
00:03:45,820 --> 00:03:48,540
They also ask what matters most to the
person.

54
00:03:49,320 --> 00:03:53,900
The conversation can cover likely symptom
relief, whether the proposed treatment may

55
00:03:53,960 --> 00:03:58,280
help the person live longer, the chance of
needing another procedure, and the risks

56
00:03:58,340 --> 00:04:03,320
and recovery involved. Useful questions
are, “What are you hoping this treatment

57
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:04,140
will do for me?”

58
00:04:04,540 --> 00:04:07,320
and “What alternatives fit my situation?”

59
00:04:07,910 --> 00:04:10,450
Watch where the blood enters each graft.

60
00:04:11,490 --> 00:04:16,490
In this first example, the surgeon uses an
artery that already runs inside the left

61
00:04:16,630 --> 00:04:19,810
chest, called the left internal thoracic
artery.

62
00:04:20,750 --> 00:04:24,270
It normally receives blood from the left
subclavian artery.

63
00:04:24,810 --> 00:04:26,750
Its upper end keeps that connection.

64
00:04:27,550 --> 00:04:31,250
Its lower end is joined to the LAD beyond
the narrowing.

65
00:04:32,310 --> 00:04:36,990
Blood can then travel from the subclavian
artery, through the graft, and into the

66
00:04:37,030 --> 00:04:42,310
downstream coronary artery. Here is a
separate example using a vein from

67
00:04:42,370 --> 00:04:44,150
the leg as a free graft.

68
00:04:45,050 --> 00:04:48,210
One end is usually joined to the ascending
aorta.

69
00:04:48,910 --> 00:04:53,270
The other joins the chosen coronary artery
beyond the narrowing.

70
00:04:54,330 --> 00:04:59,390
Oxygen-rich blood can then travel from the
aorta through the graft and into the heart

71
00:04:59,470 --> 00:05:04,710
muscle’s blood supply. In both examples,
the original plaque stays

72
00:05:04,750 --> 00:05:10,110
in place. The surgeon chooses the vessels
and number of grafts for the person’s

73
00:05:10,170 --> 00:05:12,030
anatomy and circumstances.

74
00:05:12,890 --> 00:05:18,090
Before planned surgery, the team reviews
the tests and explains the operation, the

75
00:05:18,170 --> 00:05:19,930
risks, and how to prepare.

76
00:05:20,790 --> 00:05:24,930
Follow the hospital’s instructions for
your medicines and when to stop eating and

77
00:05:24,990 --> 00:05:29,510
drinking. During bypass surgery, the
patient is under general

78
00:05:29,510 --> 00:05:34,310
anaesthesia. In a common approach, the
surgeon reaches the heart through the

79
00:05:34,350 --> 00:05:39,650
breastbone, and a heart-lung machine
temporarily pumps blood and adds oxygen

80
00:05:39,710 --> 00:05:40,950
while the heart is stopped.

81
00:05:41,850 --> 00:05:44,630
The surgeon connects the grafts to their
planned sites.

82
00:05:45,470 --> 00:05:50,310
The heart then resumes pumping, and the
team comes off the machine when it is safe

83
00:05:50,350 --> 00:05:55,470
to do so. In selected people, the surgeon
can work while the heart is

84
00:05:55,570 --> 00:05:58,450
still beating, without the heart-lung
machine.

85
00:05:59,470 --> 00:06:02,990
A working graft gives blood a new route to
the heart muscle.

86
00:06:03,750 --> 00:06:06,330
Some people have less angina after
recovery.

87
00:06:06,970 --> 00:06:11,110
The benefit depends on why surgery was
recommended and which areas of the heart

88
00:06:11,150 --> 00:06:15,690
are supplied. The team also discusses
risks such as bleeding,

89
00:06:15,969 --> 00:06:21,050
infection, an irregular heartbeat, kidney
problems, heart attack or stroke.

90
00:06:21,930 --> 00:06:25,570
Bypass treats the blood-flow problem in
selected arteries.

91
00:06:26,410 --> 00:06:31,410
Coronary artery disease can still
progress, and grafts can narrow or block

92
00:06:31,470 --> 00:06:35,870
over time. Follow-up and prevention remain
part of care.

93
00:06:36,830 --> 00:06:41,610
After surgery, staff often monitor
breathing, heart rhythm and blood pressure

94
00:06:41,650 --> 00:06:46,970
in intensive care. You may have temporary
breathing support and tubes that drain

95
00:06:47,090 --> 00:06:48,590
fluid from around the chest.

96
00:06:49,050 --> 00:06:52,810
The team manages pain and helps you start
moving when it is safe.

97
00:06:53,570 --> 00:06:58,610
At discharge, ask for a written plan for
wound care, medicines, activity,

98
00:06:58,930 --> 00:07:00,530
driving and follow-up.

99
00:07:01,530 --> 00:07:05,430
Recovery varies, so use the advice your
own team gives you.

100
00:07:06,210 --> 00:07:10,830
Cardiac rehabilitation can help you build
activity safely and support longer-term

101
00:07:10,890 --> 00:07:15,790
prevention. Keep taking prescribed
medicines and discuss any changes with

102
00:07:15,830 --> 00:07:16,590
your care team.

103
00:07:17,510 --> 00:07:22,110
Call your care team promptly if the wound
becomes more painful, red, or starts

104
00:07:22,190 --> 00:07:24,310
draining, or if you develop a fever.

105
00:07:25,050 --> 00:07:28,710
Call your local emergency number right
away for sudden chest pressure or

106
00:07:28,750 --> 00:07:33,550
discomfort that does not go away, or signs
of a stroke such as face droop, arm

107
00:07:33,610 --> 00:07:35,150
weakness or trouble speaking.

108
00:07:36,010 --> 00:07:40,550
As you go home, make sure you know who to
contact and when your next appointment is.

109
00:07:41,390 --> 00:07:43,710
Bypass creates a new route for blood.

110
00:07:44,450 --> 00:07:48,550
The choice starts with the person’s
symptoms, the pattern of disease, and what

111
00:07:48,630 --> 00:07:50,790
each treatment is likely to achieve.

112
00:07:52,010 --> 00:07:56,530
This is the first episode of the Elysian
Biomedical Cardiac Surgery Education

113
00:07:56,650 --> 00:07:57,210
Series.
